-- A page of reads costs the same on every call, however many days the log keeps (R48). `log_reads_by` (0049) and `log_reads_at` (0050) read each day's partition by a query whose bounds are the function's variables. PL/pgSQL plans such a query for the values of each of its first five calls, pruning `log_reads` to that day at plan time; on the sixth, Postgres also plans it once for any values, to weigh whether to keep that generic plan, and that plan covers every day partition and each of their indexes: at 106 days, 23,000 buffers in `log_reads_by` and 30,000 in `log_reads_at`, where a call reads 19 and 35, on each connection; and `log_reads_at` keeps it, so that each day made (or anything else that invalidates it) has its next call plan it again, 7,000. A page measured on such a call read 654 pages where it read 250 before (T166's member page, W308). Planned for its own values each call, a page reads its own days' partitions alone. Additive: the previous release calls the same functions, and their bodies are unchanged.
ALTER FUNCTION log_reads_by(text, bigint, bigint, integer) SET plan_cache_mode = force_custom_plan;--> statement-breakpoint
ALTER FUNCTION log_reads_at(uuid, text, bigint, bigint, integer) SET plan_cache_mode = force_custom_plan;
